      "completion_tokens": 1095,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **H₂O**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the very few substances on Earth that is naturally found in all three physical states:\n*   **Solid:** As ice, it floats on liquid water (a unique property that allows life to survive under ice).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we use for drinking, washing, and transportation.\n*   **Gas:** As water vapor, it is invisible and rises into the atmosphere.\n\n### 2. A Polar Molecule\nWater is a \"polar\" molecule. Because oxygen is more electronegative (it pulls electrons more strongly) than hydrogen, the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water forms droplets and can travel up plants against gravity.\n\n### 3. The Universal Solvent\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This property is essential for biological processes, as it allows nutrients to be transported through the bloodstream and plants to absorb minerals from the soil.\n\n### 4. High Heat Capacity\nWater can absorb and release a large amount of heat without changing temperature much. This is why the ocean moderates the Earth's climate (keeping land near the ocean cooler in the summer and warmer in the winter) and why it is crucial for regulating body temperature in living organisms.\n\n### 5. Essential for Life\nWater is the foundation of life on Earth. It is a key component of all known living cells and is necessary for:\n*   Chemical reactions in the body.\n*   Transporting nutrients and waste.\n*   Lubricating joints and organs.\n*   Cooling the body through sweating."
